      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Â© 2013 Asian Oceanian Association for the Study of Obesity . Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "We divided subjects into hs-CRP of ≤1.0 mg/l and >1.0 mg/l, and investigated the relationship between hs-CRP and basic attributes, lifestyle and health checkup test results. In particular, hs-CRP increased significantly as BMI increased, and hs-CRP of >1.0 mg/l was seen in about 40% of people with BMI of ≥25 kg/m(2) and 75% with BMI of ≥ 30 kg/m(2). Persons with 3 or more abnormalities in BMI, blood pressure, serum lipid and glucose were found in 20.0% of those with hs-CRP >1.0 mg/l, while 4.3% in hs-CRP ≤ 1.0 mg/l. The present findings have suggested that hs-CRP > 1.0 mg/l can be an indicator for obesity-related risks in male Japanese."
      },
